WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.160
Hello and welcome back to JD Science.

2
00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:07.680
This is part one of our level 3 lesson

3
00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:10.840
on the transmission of a nerve impulse.

4
00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:15.480
A nerve impulse is a brief electrical signal that travels

5
00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:16.440
along a neuron.

6
00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:20.780
A neuron is a specialized nerve cell that

7
00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:24.620
carries information from one place in the body to

8
00:00:24.620 --> 00:00:25.040
another.

9
00:00:25.540 --> 00:00:29.380
Look first at a real stained motor neuron from

10
00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:30.460
the spinal cord.

11
00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:34.560
The large purple cell bodies and the surrounding fibers

12
00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:36.220
are genuine tissue.

13
00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:41.140
A light micrograph can show the cell body in processes, but

14
00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:43.660
it cannot show the ions that create the impulse.

15
00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:47.820
So we now switch to a labeled scientific diagram.

16
00:00:48.460 --> 00:00:52.060
The cell body contains the nucleus and

17
00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:53.500
most of the cytoplasm.

18
00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:58.320
Short, branched endrites receive incoming signals and

19
00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:00.220
pass them towards the cell body.

20
00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:04.880
The axon is a long process that carries the impulse

21
00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:08.040
away from the cell body towards another cell.

22
00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:12.740
The axon is bounded by a plasma membrane, which

23
00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:15.640
is a phospholipid bilayer with proteins in it.

24
00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:19.560
In many neurons, a myelin sheath wraps around

25
00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:20.240
the axon.

26
00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:25.020
Myelin is made by Schwann cells in the peripheral nervous

27
00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:28.400
system and acts as an electrical insulator.

28
00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:32.520
The gaps between adjacent Schwann cells are the

29
00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:33.680
nodes of Ranveer.

30
00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:39.120
At each node, the membrane is exposed to the

31
00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:40.460
extracellular fluid.

32
00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:44.920
Those nodes will matter when we look at how the impulse travels

33
00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:45.480
quickly.

34
00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:49.760
A nerve impulse is an action potential traveling along the

35
00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:50.180
axon.

36
00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:54.860
An action potential is a rapid, temporary reversal of

37
00:01:54.860 --> 00:01:57.280
the electrical charge across the membrane.

38
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:01.700
Before that reversal can happen, we must understand the

39
00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:02.440
resting state.

40
00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:07.420
At rest, the inside of the axon is negative compared

41
00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:08.560
with the outside.

42
00:02:09.160 --> 00:02:12.820
That potential difference is the resting potential, about

43
00:02:12.820 --> 00:02:14.360
minus 70 millivolts.

44
00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:18.900
The unit is the millivolts, 1 ,000th

45
00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:19.660
of a volt.

46
00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:23.980
Because opposite charges are separated across the membrane, we

47
00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:25.580
say the membrane is polarized.

48
00:02:26.440 --> 00:02:28.180
The ions are not evenly shared.

49
00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:32.580
Sodium, ions, shown in amber are far more

50
00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:34.500
concentrated outside the axon.

51
00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:39.000
Potassium ions, shown in Tio are far

52
00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:40.640
more concentrated inside.

53
00:02:41.540 --> 00:02:45.520
Large negative organic ions, such as proteins, remain

54
00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:48.460
inside, because they cannot cross the membrane.

55
00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:53.100
The sodium, potassium pump, maintains those

56
00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:55.080
concentration gradients.

57
00:02:55.640 --> 00:02:59.660
It is a membrane protein that uses ATP, the cell's

58
00:02:59.660 --> 00:03:00.580
energy currency.

59
00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:05.540
For each cycle, three sodium ions are moved out, and

60
00:03:05.540 --> 00:03:07.500
two potassium ions are moved in.

61
00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:12.000
That is active transport against their concentration

62
00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:12.740
gradient.

63
00:03:13.480 --> 00:03:17.660
Because three positive ions leave for every two that enter, the

64
00:03:17.660 --> 00:03:20.780
pump also makes the inside a little more negative.

65
00:03:21.560 --> 00:03:25.420
The membrane is selectively permeable, which means it does not treat every

66
00:03:25.420 --> 00:03:26.440
ion the same way.

67
00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:31.280
At rest, it is much more permeable to potassium than

68
00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:32.060
to sodium.

69
00:03:32.920 --> 00:03:36.900
Potassium leak channels stay open, so some potassium ions

70
00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:39.400
diffuse out down their concentration gradient.

71
00:03:39.980 --> 00:03:43.880
As those positive ions leave, the inside becomes

72
00:03:43.880 --> 00:03:44.800
more negative.

73
00:03:45.700 --> 00:03:49.600
Sodium channels are mostly closed at rest, so sodium cannot easily

74
00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:50.340
flood back in.

75
00:03:51.060 --> 00:03:52.780
Put those ideas together.

76
00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:57.900
The pump keeps sodium high outside and potassium

77
00:03:57.900 --> 00:03:58.860
high inside.

78
00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:04.700
Potassium leak channels let some potassium leave.

79
00:04:05.720 --> 00:04:09.560
And in permanent negative ions stay inside.

80
00:04:10.220 --> 00:04:14.020
The result is a polarized membrane at about minus 70

81
00:04:14.020 --> 00:04:14.760
millivolts.

82
00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:17.560
That is the resting potential.

83
00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:21.300
The pump does not fire the impulse.

84
00:04:22.100 --> 00:04:25.940
It quietly maintains the gradients that make

85
00:04:25.940 --> 00:04:27.280
an impulse possible.

86
00:04:28.060 --> 00:04:30.640
Here is a worked examination style example.

87
00:04:31.300 --> 00:04:35.020
A student writes the sodium potassium pump creates

88
00:04:35.020 --> 00:04:38.620
the resting potential by pumping sodium in and

89
00:04:38.620 --> 00:04:39.400
potassium out.

90
00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:43.360
Identify two scientific errors and correct them.

91
00:04:44.060 --> 00:04:46.120
The first error is the direction.

92
00:04:46.880 --> 00:04:50.640
The pump moves three sodium ions out, not in and

93
00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:52.980
two potassium ions in, not out.

94
00:04:53.720 --> 00:04:57.360
The second error is the claim that the pump alone creates the

95
00:04:57.360 --> 00:04:58.300
resting potential.

96
00:04:59.560 --> 00:05:02.560
The pump maintains the gradients.

97
00:05:03.260 --> 00:05:07.160
Potassium leak channels and the trapped negative ions are

98
00:05:07.160 --> 00:05:08.360
also essential.

99
00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:13.420
Now pause the video and try the sequencing question.

100
00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:17.600
Place these four statements in the order that establishes

101
00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:18.800
a resting potential.

102
00:05:19.700 --> 00:05:23.700
A, the sodium potassium pump uses

103
00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:25.440
ATPP B.

104
00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:29.920
Sodium stays high outside and potassium stays high

105
00:05:29.920 --> 00:05:30.280
inside.

106
00:05:31.160 --> 00:05:34.780
C, potassium leaks out through

107
00:05:34.780 --> 00:05:36.100
open leak channels.

108
00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:40.600
Day, the membrane is polarized at about minus

109
00:05:40.600 --> 00:05:41.840
70 millivolts.

110
00:05:42.540 --> 00:05:44.760
Here is a model answer.

111
00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:48.940
First day, the pump uses ATPP.

112
00:05:49.480 --> 00:05:53.800
That produces B, the unequal ion distribution.

113
00:05:54.340 --> 00:05:58.060
Then C, potassium leaks out.

114
00:05:58.520 --> 00:06:02.540
That produces D, a polarized membrane at

115
00:06:02.540 --> 00:06:04.540
about minus 70 millivolts.

116
00:06:05.140 --> 00:06:09.060
In part two, we will see how a stimulus changes this polarized

117
00:06:09.060 --> 00:06:11.520
membrane into an action potential.

118
00:06:12.180 --> 00:06:15.020
And we will build the graph as each phase happens.

119
00:06:15.940 --> 00:06:19.560
If this explanation has helped, please subscribe to JD

120
00:06:19.560 --> 00:06:20.940
Science for more lessons.

121
00:06:21.640 --> 00:06:25.900
You can find further learning resources and practice questions at JDScience

122
00:06:25.900 --> 00:06:27.960
.co .uk.

123
00:06:28.960 --> 00:06:29.920
Thank you for watching.
